Quoting  Byron A Jeff <byron@cc.gatech.edu>:
>Since you don't play the protection racket that means you get divine
>protection later. What I want to know is what do you do to manage AC
>in the meantime and how do you manage the negative effects?

Let's confine ourselves to talking about spellcasters; obviously
combat-wombats suffer less from weight and worsened failure percentages.

First of all, I don't think the weight problem is that critical. In the
early game I am generally not trying to carry much stuff around; scrolls
and potions are being squirreled away upstairs for later identification.
Of course the big non-mithril armours are extremely heavy, but you can
squeeze out a fair bit of AC without touching those, and there's no reason
why you should end up Burdened with proper luggage discipline.

Secondly, there's more approaches than AC. Another approach is not to get
attacked in the first place; we're back to that old favourite, ranged
attacks. As a spellcaster, if I have any offensive capabilities, I can use
them more effectively if I'm levelling up - increased mana, better failure
rates, and more damage on some attacks. _If_ I can use those attacks
effectively and avoid point-blank range, I think levelling up can help me
more than it helps the monsters. (Another thing that it's easy to forget
is that there's no prize for dying with the most unused attack wand
charges).

Looking back through my records, I seem to have ascended a wizard with an
involuntary banzai charge into the Mines caused by an angry shopkeeper.
She was wearing studded leather, elven leather helm, leather gloves, high
boots, her CoMR, and GoDex. I am pretty sure she bought _some_ protection
at Minetown - an important point is that, when you clean out all the
shopkeepers (modulo Izchak, obviously), you've got enough gold to get some
protection even if you've been levelling up.  Shopkeepers start with an
average of 2500 gold; so you get the high value first buy and probably a
second or third bite, reducing the expected net gain from the racket.
That's 8 points of physical AC, 3 points of protection (average from 2
buys), and obviously some of that gear had plusses on it because I
pet-test everything. That puts me far enough in the negatives to render
soldier ants and other monsters with fast weak attacks relatively
harmless.

She also seems to have made extensive use of pets. Of course, if you're
killing stuff and eating corpses, you can burn food items on taming cats
and dogs - if I'm not a wombat, I try and have a string of 3 or 4 pets.
That's very valuable, and it's harder to do if you can't kill anything
yourself; particularly since little dogs and kittens have a MONSTR of 3. A
classic XL 1 protectioneer will have to get to DL 5 before they start to
be generated; even your approach reduces the number you can expect to see,
I think. Pets are a common element; my 3.4.0 monk polytrapped his cat into
an orange dragon.
